1
00:00:01,833 --> 00:00:05,833
An index gives PostgreSQL another way to find rows.
Whether it helps depends on the query and the data.

2
00:00:08,800 --> 00:00:14,167
The table is not ordered by last name.

3
00:00:14,533 --> 00:00:17,533
Two rows match. Finding the first one does not end the scan.

4
00:00:23,333 --> 00:00:27,333
The B-tree keeps ordered keys and row locations.
Creating it does not reorder the table.

5
00:00:29,933 --> 00:00:33,333
A B-tree uses guide keys to find the matching key range.

6
00:00:33,700 --> 00:00:38,300
The index finds two locations. SELECT * also needs fname and id,
so this plan fetches the complete rows from the table.

7
00:00:38,667 --> 00:00:42,667
Same matching rows; another route.
For just six rows, PostgreSQL may still choose a sequential scan.

8
00:00:45,667 --> 00:00:49,867
This query asks for all columns of all rows.
The lname index cannot eliminate rows from that request.

9
00:00:50,233 --> 00:00:54,233
Reading the table directly is often cheaper than
walking an index and fetching every table row.

10
00:00:56,267 --> 00:01:00,067
PostgreSQL reads table pages. On a tiny table,
a direct scan can cost less than an index lookup plus row fetches.

11
00:01:00,433 --> 00:01:07,433
If a matching index lets a query visit only a few table pages,
it can avoid much of the scan. These boxes are illustrative pages.

12
00:01:07,800 --> 00:01:11,800
When matches spread across most pages, index-driven row fetches
may add work. A sequential scan or bitmap plan may cost less.

13
00:01:14,600 --> 00:01:19,400
A first-name index does not directly locate a last-name match.
The ON users (lname) definition determines the indexed column.

14
00:01:19,767 --> 00:01:22,767
users_lname_idx is a helpful name.
The index name itself does not control which column it searches.

15
00:01:26,000 --> 00:01:30,000
The insert maintains both structures.
Deletes and changes to indexed values also require index work.

16
00:01:30,367 --> 00:01:33,367
Keep an index when its benefit to the workload
is worth its extra storage and maintenance.

17
00:01:36,900 --> 00:01:40,900
Compare actual plan, rows, and buffers before and after.
Keep the query and data the same; confirm the same results.

18
00:01:41,267 --> 00:01:45,267
PostgreSQL chooses a plan using estimated costs.
An unchanged sequential scan can be a sensible result.
